# ARCHITECTURE.md

# Palisade Architecture

## Overview

Palisade is a Godot 4 C# first-person dungeon/maze game. It uses a 2D piece-based map editor to define dungeon layouts, then generates a playable 3D dungeon from those layouts.

The game is built around modular maze pieces that must connect naturally through openings. Some pieces are flat rooms/corridors, while others are stairs that connect multiple levels.

## Tech Stack

- Engine: Godot 4.x
- Language: C#
- Project file: `project.godot`
- Runtime scripts: `scripts/*.cs`
- Scenes: `scenes/*.tscn`

## Core Flow

1. The user places pieces in the 2D map editor.
2. Each piece stores type/value, position, and rotation.
3. Piece openings are checked so neighboring pieces connect legally.
4. Map data is saved/loaded through serializer logic.
5. On game start or generation, the dungeon builder reads the map layout.
6. The dungeon builder creates 3D floors, walls, ramps, collisions, and center arena connections.
7. The player spawns into the generated dungeon.

## System Responsibilities

### Map Editor

Main files:

- `scripts/MapEditorMain.cs`
- `scripts/TileData.cs`
- `scripts/MapSerializer.cs`

Responsibilities:

- piece selection
- piece placement
- piece rotation
- layout editing
- layout save/load
- preparing data for generation

### Piece Database

Main file:

- `scripts/PieceDB.cs`

Responsibilities:

- defining piece types
- returning openings
- handling rotation
- stair up/down direction
- determining connection metadata

Important risk:
If piece openings or stair directions are wrong here, validation and 3D generation will both behave incorrectly.

### Map Validation

Main file:

- `scripts/MapValidator.cs`

Responsibilities:

- checking whether adjacent pieces connect correctly
- rejecting impossible layouts
- protecting generator from invalid map data

### Dungeon Builder

Main file:

- `scripts/DungeonBuilder.cs`

Responsibilities:

- converting tile layout into 3D geometry
- creating floors, walls, ramps, and collisions
- handling stair geometry
- filling blocked walls
- leaving valid openings clear
- stitching maps to the center arena / middle room

Important risk:
Most visible generation bugs probably live here, especially missing walls, wrong openings, bad collisions, and arena stitching.

### Multi-cell Pieces (Room, Pit)

Main files:

- `scripts/PieceDB.cs` (`Footprint`, `FloorSpan`, `IsMultiCell`)
- `scripts/RoomGroups.cs`
- `scripts/RoomBuilder.cs`
- `scripts/PitBuilder.cs`

Most pieces occupy exactly one `(X, Y, Floor)` cell. `Room` and `Pit` are
built differently, and any future piece that also spans more than one cell
should follow the same contract rather than inventing its own:

- **`Room`** is stored as one independent `MazePiece` per cell — painting a
  rectangle in the editor writes N separate single-cell `Room` tiles, not one
  rectangle object. `Room` declares openings on all four faces at every
  rotation, so every existing per-tile system (validator BFS, serializer,
  gold cost, single-cell deletion) already handles it correctly with no
  changes. `RoomGroups.Build` flood-fills adjacent `Room` tiles into
  connected `RoomGroup`s at build/render time only — **`RoomGroups` is the
  seam between per-tile storage and per-space geometry.** Nothing upstream of
  it (editor, validator, serializer) ever sees a "room" as more than a
  scatter of 1x1 tiles; `RoomBuilder.Build` is the only place that turns a
  `RoomGroup` back into one merged floor, perimeter-only walls, and one
  continuous vault.
- **`Pit`** is a genuinely multi-cell piece: one `MazePiece` at the shaft's
  centre (its anchor) represents a 3x3 footprint spanning up to 3 floors.
  `PieceDB.Footprint(t)` returns the cell offsets from the anchor and
  `PieceDB.FloorSpan(t)` returns the floor offsets, both clipped to the
  world's floor range at the point of use; `PieceDB.IsMultiCell(t)` is true
  only for `Pit` today.

Both types share ONE doorway treatment, `RoomBuilder.ArchedDoorHead` (with
`RoomBuilder.DoorJambY` for the flanking walls beside it). A corridor's opening is
not a rectangle: it is `OpeningW` wide and rises `CellHeight` flat then arches to
`FloorHeight`. Any space a corridor opens into must cut its threshold to that same
profile, clipped to whatever ceiling that space actually has at the opening, and
emit the strip above the head into a SECOND surface wound outward — the corridor,
not the room, is what looks at that strip. A flat lintel there leaves the top of the
hallway arch unsealed and, because the panel faces inward, backface culling makes
the hole see-through. Any future piece a corridor can open into should route its
doorway head through the same helper rather than inventing a lintel.

**`PieceDB.Footprint` / `PieceDB.FloorSpan` are the only sanctioned way to ask
what volume a piece claims.** Editor occupancy
(`MazeEditor3D.TryGetPlacedPieceOnFloor`), placement validation
(`MazeEditor3D.IsValidPlacement`), and validator connectivity
(`MazeValidator.IsGameReady`'s BFS) all resolve a piece's volume through these
two methods rather than hard-coding 3x3/3-floor math of their own. A future
multi-cell piece type that implements `Footprint` and `FloorSpan` inherits
editor occupancy and BFS connectivity for free — no other system needs to
change. Both methods return a **fresh copy** of their backing array on every
call; do not change them to return a shared reference — an earlier version did
exactly that, and one caller mutating the result in place corrupted every
other piece type's footprint for the rest of the process's life.

Geometry for both piece types is emitted **per (cell, floor)**, one
`StaticBody3D`/mesh set per cell (matching `DungeonBuilder.BuildFloor`'s
convention), never one mesh merged across a whole room group or shaft — this
renderer's GL-compatibility backend lights a mesh from its bounding box centre,
so a large merged mesh leaves its corners dark. Continuity across cell borders
(the room vault, the pit's dome) is preserved by always computing the
arch/vault height from the *group's* or *shaft's* world-space bounds, never a
per-cell offset, so adjacent cells agree on height at their shared edge with
no seam.

Important risk: any decoration pass that keys off a piece's stored
`(X, Y, Floor)` — e.g. `PieceVariants`, which looks up a body by
`Cell_{p.X}_{p.Y}_{p.Floor}` — must explicitly account for `IsMultiCell`
pieces, since the anchor cell does not necessarily have real floor geometry
under it (a `Pit`'s anchor floor is usually its open middle level). Any
editor action that can implicitly delete a piece (painting over it, dropping
another piece onto it) must check for an occupied multi-cell footprint first
and refuse, the same way `Start`/`Exit` are protected — this editor has no
undo.

### Traps

Main files:

- `scripts/PieceDB.cs` (`Colors`, `Labels`, `ShortLabels`, `GoldCosts`, `Category`
  entries for each trap `PieceType`)
- `scripts/TrapDB.cs` (glyph + editor color lookup, tuning constants)
- `scripts/TrapZone.cs` (`Area3D` movement-multiplier bag)
- `scripts/TrapBuilder.cs` (dressing geometry, `AddZone` wiring, dispatch)
- `scripts/TrapBehaviours.cs` (Darkness, Trapdoor, One-Way Gate, Teleport Pad
  behaviour nodes)

Eight trap `PieceType`s exist today: `TrapIce`, `TrapTar`, `TrapDark`,
`TrapDoor`, `TrapGate`, `TrapPush`, `TrapPortal`, `TrapGrease`. **Design
constraint every trap holds to: no trap deals damage.** The game is a timed
escape, so a trap's cost is always the opponent's clock, never their health —
this is stated in `TrapDB.cs`'s header comment and should be treated as a
constraint on any new trap, not a suggestion.

A new trap is exactly three things, and adding a ninth trap means touching
only these three, never the validator, the builder's piece-placement code, or
the editor's connection rules:

1. **A `PieceType` with `Dir.N | Dir.S` openings** in `PieceDB.BaseOpenings`,
   plus the matching entries in `Colors`/`Labels`/`ShortLabels`/`GoldCosts`/
   `Category` (`PieceCategory.Traps`). The fixed N|S shape is what makes
   `PieceDB.GetOpenings`, `MazeValidator`'s BFS, `DungeonBuilder.AddPiece`,
   and the editor's placement rules treat the piece as an ordinary `Straight`
   corridor with no code changes anywhere in that chain — a trap only ever
   adds dressing and behaviour on top of geometry that already builds itself
   correctly.
2. **A `TrapDB` entry**: a `TrapGlyph` (the editor thumbnail/grid-tile mark,
   drawn by `MazeEditor3D.BuildTrapGlyph`), and inclusion in `TrapDB.IsTrap`
   (checked via the `Glyphs` dictionary) and, if the piece's rotation changes
   its *behaviour* and not just its geometry, `TrapDB.IsDirectional` (true
   today only for `TrapGate`/`TrapPush` — both grow a directional arrow on
   their editor tile via `BuildTrapGlyph`). Any tuning numbers the trap needs
   live here as named constants, not inline magic numbers in the behaviour
   code.
3. **Either a `TrapZone` configuration or a `TrapBehaviours` node.**
   `TrapBuilder.Decorate` is the single dispatch point (called once per
   non-stair piece from `DungeonBuilder.BuildFloor`); its switch either
   configures a `TrapZone` directly (Ice, Tar, Push, Grease — see below) or
   falls through to `TrapBehaviours.Decorate` for a trap that needs real
   state (Darkness, Trapdoor, Gate, Teleport). A trap with no state — it only
   ever needs to modulate movement while the player is standing in a volume —
   should be a `TrapZone` configuration, not a bespoke node; only reach for
   `TrapBehaviours` when the trap has a timer, a one-shot latch, or needs to
   reference another node (a partner pad, a hatch).

**`TrapZone` effects combine multiplicatively (and, for booleans, with OR)
across every zone the player currently overlaps**, so overlapping traps stack
for free without either zone knowing the other exists — standing in ice
inside a darkness zone gets both effects simultaneously.
`PlayerController.RecomputeTrapModifiers` folds the player's `_trapZones` set
down to a small number of multipliers
(`_trapFrictionMul`/`_trapSpeedMul`/`_trapAccelMul`/`_trapPush`/
`_trapNoWallRun`) every time a zone is entered or exited; five call sites in
`PlayerController.cs` (top speed, ground acceleration, ground friction, a
push impulse applied just before `MoveAndSlide()`, and the wall-run start
gate) read those multipliers. With no `TrapZone` overlapping the player every
multiplier is exactly `1f` (push `Vector3.Zero`, wall-run block `false`), so
each read is a mathematical or boolean identity — normal movement is
unaffected when no trap is present. A `TrapZone`'s trigger volume is
`CellHeight * 0.9f` tall (~12.6 m, not a floor-flush slab) specifically so a
bhopping player crossing a trap cell mid-air still counts as inside it —
jumping across a trap must not be a way to skip it.

Important risks:

- **`MazeEditor3D.Palette` is a hand-maintained array, separate from
  `PieceDB`, and nothing automatically keeps it in sync.** A `PieceType` can
  be fully wired into `PieceDB` (openings, color, labels, gold cost,
  category) and still never appear as a placeable tile if it isn't also
  added to `Palette` — this happened to all eight traps (and `Room`/`Pit`
  before them). `test/TestPieceCategories.cs` now asserts every `PieceType`
  is either in `Palette` or in an explicit exclusion list
  (`Start`/`Exit`/legacy `Stairs`), so adding a new piece type without a
  palette decision fails a headless test instead of shipping an empty tab.
  Any new trap (or any new piece type at all) must add itself to `Palette`
  and let that test confirm it.
- **The `"Floor"` mesh-name prefix is load-bearing.** `DungeonBuilder.AddMesh`
  picks floor vs. wall material by `name.StartsWith("Floor")`, and
  `TrapBehaviours.AddTrapdoor` relies on that same prefix to find and hide
  the corridor floor mesh/collision it replaces with a hatch. Any future
  trap (or any piece code at all) that emits its own floor mesh must name it
  with the `"Floor"` prefix literally first, matching the existing Room/Pit
  convention (`FloorRoom_`/`FloorPit_`, not `RoomFloor_`/`PitFloor_`).
- **A `TrapZone`/behaviour trigger must stay a pure `Area3D`, never a
  `StaticBody3D`-backed collider.** All dressing geometry
  (`TrapBuilder.AddFloorOverlay`, `AddWallSheen`, the Darkness curtains) is
  visual only, with no `CollisionShape3D` of its own — the only physical
  effect a trap has on how a cell walks must come through `TrapZone` or a
  `TrapBehaviours` node's own trigger, never from a dressing mesh
  accidentally picking up collision by being added through
  `DungeonBuilder.AddMesh` instead of a bare `MeshInstance3D`.
- **`TrapGate`'s `Dir.N | Dir.S` declaration in `PieceDB.BaseOpenings` is a
  runtime fiction.** To the validator and the editor it presents as an
  ordinary bidirectional `Straight` — same as every other trap, per point 1
  above — but `OneWayGateNode` actually behaves as a directed, latching edge:
  `OnEnter` only arms when the player's velocity travels WITH `Facing`, and
  `OnExit` (since the I3 fix) only shuts the gate when the player's exit
  position lies on the far side of `Facing`, never on backing out the near
  side. `MazeValidator`'s BFS has no notion of a directed edge at all, so it
  certifies a maze as solvable while treating a `TrapGate` cell as freely
  traversable both ways — which it is, right up until a player commits to
  the legal direction and passes all the way through, at which point that
  edge becomes one-way for the rest of the run. Any future change to gate
  behaviour must preserve this: the piece's *declared* openings must stay
  symmetric (that's what keeps it playable by every other subsystem), while
  its *actual* traversal is enforced entirely inside `OneWayGateNode`.

### Player Controller

Main file:

- `scripts/Player.cs`

Responsibilities:

- first-person movement
- third-person toggle
- wall running
- bunny hopping
- momentum
- sliding
- sword combat
- block
- aerial spin attack
- debug controls

### Menu Chrome

Main file:

- `scripts/MenuUI.cs`

Owns the shared chrome for the full-screen menu tabs (`scenes/PlayGameScreen.cs`,
`scenes/PreGameLobby.cs`, `scenes/TrainingSetupScreen.cs`).

`MenuUI.AddBackButton(screen, font, handler)` is the only way a menu tab should
create its "leave this screen" button. It anchors the button to the screen root's
top-left corner at `CornerPad` and applies the shared red. Tabs must pass their
SCREEN ROOT, not a flow container — anchoring to the root is what keeps the
button on the same pixel regardless of how the tab lays out its own content, and
the call must come last in the tab's UI build so the button draws on top.

Deliberately does NOT cover the overlay cards (`scripts/PauseMenu.cs`,
`scripts/SettingsPanel.cs`). Those are small centred panels, so a
screen-anchored corner would place their Back/Close outside the card.

### Online Match (OnlineDungeonArena + NetworkManager)

- Dumb WebSocket relay (server/relay.js, deployed on Render) forwards JSON
  between exactly two clients; `type` = relay/lobby messages, `t` = game
  messages. Damage is client-authoritative: the shooter resolves hits locally
  and relays `{"t":"hit"}`.
- **Match start gate**: both clients spawn Frozen behind a MatchCountdown
  overlay, exchange `arena_ready`, then run 3-2-1-GO locally (skew ≤ one-way
  latency). Combat is LIVE from GO (truce removed 2026-09-22); the middle
  claim starts the 40 s hunt, roles, and the wallhack/diamond marking.
- **Loadout**: host rolls starter+arena once and broadcasts; the roll is
  buffered client-side (`_rolledStarterIdx`) because the message can beat the
  arena build. Starter = SECONDARY slot, arena prize = PRIMARY; both are kept
  and Tab swaps (`GunCombat.SwitchTo` preserves per-gun magazines).
- **Hit claims carry evidence**: `hit` includes where the shooter saw the
  victim (the victim's own last-broadcast position, not the extrapolated
  render). The victim validates against a 750 ms ring buffer of its real
  positions (1.5 m tolerance) — normal latency passes, stale/fabricated
  claims are rejected.
- **CombatTeam is load-bearing**: GunCombat's friendly-fire check silently
  eats same-team shots, and every gun defaults to team 0 — any spawned
  opponent (puppet, bot) MUST be assigned a different team or PvP damage
  cannot exist. The arena sets `_puppet.Gun.CombatTeam = 1` at spawn.
- **Puppet rendering**: player bodies live on visual layer 2 (culled by the
  owner's FP camera). The body layer has TWO writers — spawn and the
  per-equip TP-weapon rebuild — and both must resolve `IsRemotePuppet ? 1 : 2`
  or the opponent turns invisible on their next weapon change.

## Current Design Pillars

- First person by default
- Third person toggle on `3`
- Fast expressive movement
- Sword found in maze center before combat unlocks
- Stylized realistic retro dungeon vibe
- Modular, grid-aligned dungeon pieces
- Multi-level maze support through stair pieces

## Known Architecture Risks

- Stair pieces are more complex than flat pieces because they involve rotation, openings, height, and ramp geometry.
- Center arena stitching depends on consistent coordinate/origin math.
- Handmade maps and generated maps must use the same piece scale and connection rules.
- Collision must be generated alongside visuals, not as an afterthought.
